WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.462
Milvus and Zilliz are commonly discussed for larger vector-search workloads.

00:00:01.462 --> 00:00:02.362
The pattern is still the same.

00:00:02.362 --> 00:00:05.127
Create or connect to a collection, define the vector dimension and metric,

00:00:05.127 --> 00:00:07.442
insert vectors with associated fields, and search using the query vector.

00:00:07.442 --> 00:00:10.102
The classroom example focuses on the search pattern rather than a full production deployment.

00:00:10.102 --> 00:00:12.617
At larger scale, additional concerns appear: index type, partitioning, replication, memory usage,

00:00:12.617 --> 00:00:13.572
deployment topology, and operational monitoring.

00:00:13.572 --> 00:00:15.432
Those are important topics, but they come after the basic RAG architecture is clear.

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:17.867
I want you to see that even when the infrastructure becomes more sophisticated,

00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:20.552
the application still needs good chunks, appropriate embeddings, useful metadata,

00:00:20.552 --> 00:00:21.832
and a way to evaluate retrieval quality.

00:00:21.832 --> 00:00:22.993
Scale does not replace the fundamentals.
